      "problem": "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}+1},\\,{t}^{5}⟩,$ ${r}^{′}(t)=$",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}+1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{{e}^{t}({e}^{t}+1)-{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)={t}^{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=5{t}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨{x}^{′}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{t}+1)}^{2}},\\,5{t}^{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
